From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Copilot <copilot@github.com>
Date: Sat, 07 Mar 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: Pre-enable CLKCR clock output after controller reset

After sunxi_mmc_probe() issues SUNXI_MMC_GCTRL_RESET, all controller
registers (including CLKCR) are reset to their hardware defaults, so
CLKCR.CLK_ENABLE becomes 0 (clock output disabled).

mmc_set_initial_state() calls mmc_set_clock(mmc, 0, MMC_CLK_ENABLE).
mmc_set_clock() clamps 0 to cfg->f_min (400 kHz), so mmc->clock = 400000.
sunxi_mmc_set_ios_common() then calls mmc_config_clock(priv, 400000),
which immediately disables CLK_ENABLE and issues mmc_update_clk() with
SUNXI_MMC_CMD_WAIT_PRE_OVER.  Because CLKCR.CLK_ENABLE is still 0 at
that point the controller never drives CLK, the WAIT_PRE_OVER condition
never resolves, and the command times out after 2 seconds leaving
priv->fatal_err = 1 and making the device permanently unusable.

Fix this by setting CLKCR.CLK_ENABLE immediately after the reset so that
the first mmc_update_clk(WAIT_PRE_OVER) call inside mmc_config_clock()
can complete.  We deliberately do NOT call mmc_update_clk() here because
that would use WAIT_PRE_OVER on a freshly-reset controller whose DATA
lines may still be settling; mmc_config_clock() handles the full clock
programming sequence correctly once CLK_ENABLE is asserted.

Signed-off-by: Copilot <copilot@github.com>
---
 drivers/mmc/sunxi_mmc.c | 25 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/mmc/sunxi_mmc.c b/drivers/mmc/sunxi_mmc.c
index 3333333..4444444 100644
--- a/drivers/mmc/sunxi_mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/sunxi_mmc.c
@@ -688,7 +688,30 @@ static int sunxi_mmc_probe(struct udevice *dev)
 	/* Reset controller */
 	writel(SUNXI_MMC_GCTRL_RESET, &priv->reg->gctrl);
 	udelay(1000);
 
+	/*
+	 * Enable clock output to the card after the controller reset.
+	 * After GCTRL_RESET the controller's CLKCR register is zeroed, so
+	 * CLK_ENABLE = 0.  mmc_set_initial_state() will call mmc_config_clock()
+	 * (because mmc_set_clock() clamps 0 to f_min = 400 kHz), which first
+	 * disables the clock and calls mmc_update_clk() before re-enabling it.
+	 * That first mmc_update_clk() call uses WAIT_PRE_OVER, but CLKCR still
+	 * has CLK_ENABLE = 0 so the hardware never asserts the CLK line and the
+	 * UPCLK_ONLY command hangs for 2 seconds before timing out, setting
+	 * fatal_err = 1 and making the device permanently unusable.
+	 *
+	 * Pre-enable the clock here so that CLKCR.CLK_ENABLE = 1 when
+	 * mmc_config_clock() first calls mmc_update_clk(WAIT_PRE_OVER).
+	 * We do NOT call mmc_update_clk() ourselves here because that would
+	 * use WAIT_PRE_OVER on a just-reset controller, which may hang if
+	 * the card DATA lines are still settling.  The clock divider stays
+	 * at its reset value (0 = divide-by-1) which is fine; CLKCR is
+	 * reprogrammed completely by the subsequent mmc_config_clock() call.
+	 */
+	setbits_le32(&priv->reg->clkcr, SUNXI_MMC_CLK_ENABLE);
+
 	return 0;
 }
